<section id="A2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.3 </span>Gradient Norm Clipping Results</h3>

<div id="A2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A2.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ B.3 Gradient Norm Clipping Results ‣ Appendix B Full Experimental Results ‣ SoftAdaClip: A Smooth Clipping Strategy for Fair and Private Model Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> reports the average gradient norms before and after clipping for each subgroup. As discussed in the main text, SoftAdaClip applies less clipping than other methods due to its smooth transformation. Even Fixed Soft Clipping results in lower clipping than standard DP-SGD. An exception is the Income Simple dataset with <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> = 0.1, where the gradients are already small, leading to minimal clipping under DP-SGD. In this setting, SoftAdaClip initially offered limited benefit, as there was little suppression to improve upon. However, when we reduced the clipping threshold, the gradients became more likely to be clipped, and SoftAdaClip demonstrated its advantage by smoothing the clipping operation and improving fairness.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>Gradient statistics across all datasets and methods. Each cell shows: Before <math id="A2.T4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> After (Diff).</figcaption>
